//! Reference counts, and what a count reaching zero does.
//!
//! Every countable slab slot carries a `refs`. Maintaining it comes down to
//! three questions, each answered by an exhaustive match on `ObjType`:
//!
//!   `ref_object`     something now names this object
//!   `unref_object`   something no longer does; zero frees, right here
//!   `free_object`    the slot goes back and the kind's last rites run
//!
//! The answers differ per kind (an Interrupt masks its line when its one name
//! goes, a Timer reprograms the comparator, a Waiter detaches its list and
//! wakes what is parked on it, a Mapping deliberately leaves its protection row
//! installed), which is why this is a matrix of three functions in one place. A
//! kind added to `ObjType` fails to compile in all three until someone says
//! what references it; a `_` arm here would destroy that property.
//!
//! `PipeInlet` and `PipeOutlet` are two counted kinds over one connection slot
//! with two reference columns. `PipeReply` and `PipeRequest` are two counted
//! kinds over one exchange (ring slot) with two columns. Each arm decrements
//! its own column and then answers about the pair, so `unref_object`'s "zero
//! frees" means "both columns zero" without knowing two kinds are involved. One
//! column reaching zero is the peer-gone (or abandonment) transition.
//! `drop_reference` is the only place a column reaching zero is observed, and
//! `free_object` runs only when both are zero, so the transition cannot be
//! written anywhere else. `exchange_can_settle` carries the one extra clause: a
//! staged message is still owed to a live outlet whatever became of its claim
//! (spec §2.1's tell idiom).
//!
//! Teardown does not force-free the counted kinds other processes may still
//! reference (regions, IO windows, pipe connections, Events);
//! `reap_unreferenced` frees what nobody names and disowns the rest (see the
//! block above it). Threads, Waiters and Timers are invalidated by owner and
//! freed by count in `end_process`'s per-kind sweeps. Mappings are swept by
//! owner. An Interrupt has no owner: releasing its one handle destroys it
//! (`drop_reference`), including in the close-all, and the Interrupt sweep
//! frees the invalid ones nothing names any more.
//!
//! This file also holds delivery, readiness becoming a woken thread. The ledger
//! and delivery are mutually recursive: a delivery drops a reference (one-shot
//! and reply attachments detach at delivery), and a dropped reference delivers
//! (the peer-gone levels fire when a column reaches zero). A module boundary
//! cannot hold that cycle, and an import cycle silently empties a module's
//! export table, so both live here. The wait tables stay in `waitables`; this
//! file is the acting half.
//!
//! # Zero frees synchronously
//!
//! The kernel is one context running to completion, so the count hits zero
//! inside the syscall that dropped the last reference and the free is a plain
//! call: no deferred reclamation, no cleanup queue. Cascades compose out of the
//! same two functions (releasing a Waiter to zero detaches its attachments,
//! which may drop an Event to zero, which frees and credits). They terminate
//! because an attachment is counted on the waitable and never on the Waiter, so
//! the graph is a forest.
//!
//! Quotas count objects, not bytes, in v1 (spec §2.9); bytes are the pool's own
//! ledger (§2.5).
//!
//! # Placement
//!
//! This module sits above `process`, because delivery needs `process`'s copy
//! doors and a Process's own count drives its record's reclaim, and below
//! `sched`, which holds the teardown and imports this module. It reaches every
//! slab (the memory slabs and the handle table in `objects`, the Timer slab in
//! `time`, the wait slabs in `waitables`) and `PROCESS_RECORDS` in `pslot`, and
//! nothing above itself.
//!
//! Two mints are not expressed through this matrix because they happen in
//! `process`, below this file: a boot Memory and IoMemory are created with
//! `refs: 1` in the slot literal (a fresh object's first reference is the
//! count), and root's own Process handle is one increment on its own table.
//! Every generic site, where a handle is bound to a kind the code does not
//! know, comes through here.

